Cor Pulmonale
Cardiovascular
RV heave, Elevated JVP/Oedema, Loud P2, RV hypertrophy on echo

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CDV-013
Reason for Visit: Progressive abdominal swelling, leg heaviness, and worsening breathlessness in a patient with known chronic lung disease.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and daughter
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage highlighted significant new-onset bilateral leg swelling and worsening dyspnea in a patient with severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. This points to the development or acute exacerbation of right-sided heart failure. Urgent evaluation was required to confirm right ventricular strain, assess for acute hypoxemia or cor pulmonale decompensation, and adjust diuretic therapy, precluding safe delay until the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My breathing is getting worse, my belly feels tight and bloated, and my legs are blown up like balloons."
History of Present Illness: Patient with a known 10-year history of severe COPD reports a progressive worsening of exertional dyspnea over the past three weeks. Now notes significant, newly developing bilateral peripheral pitting edema and abdominal distension. Complains of a dull ache in the right upper abdominal quadrant. No recent purulent sputum, fever, or acute chest pain. KEY SYMPTOMS: Progressive worsening of exertional dyspnoea. Tender hepatomegaly and ascites. Significant bilateral peripheral pitting oedema. History of chronic lung disease (COPD).
Functional Status: Mobility severely restricted to the home environment; reliant on daughter for domestic tasks.
Social/Safety Concerns: Decreased mobility increasing risk of pressure injuries and falls.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/65 | HR: 94 | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 24 | SpO2: 89 percent (on room air) | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Chronically unwell appearing, barrel chest, tachypneic at rest, utilizing accessory muscles.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Elevated jugular venous pressure (6 cm above sternal angle) with a prominent v wave. Prominent right ventricular heave palpable at the left sternal border. Auscultation reveals a loud pulmonary component of the second heart sound (P2) and a right-sided third heart sound (S3). Abdomen: Distended with shifting dullness indicating mild ascites; tender hepatomegaly present. Extremities: 3+ bilateral pitting edema to the mid-thighs. Mild peripheral cyanosis noted. Respiratory: Diffuse expiratory wheeze and prolonged expiratory phase. DIAGNOSTIC TETRAD: RV heave, Elevated JVP/Oedema, Loud P2, RV hypertrophy on echo (pending formal confirmation).
Environmental Observations: Home oxygen concentrator present but patient has been non-compliant.
Medication Reconciliation: Using prescribed bronchodilators. Not currently on regular diuretics.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Cor Pulmonale (Decompensated Right Heart Failure) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Left-Sided Heart Failure - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of fine bibasal crackles and orthopnea; findings are predominantly systemic venous congestion and localized to right-heart strain indicators (RV heave, loud P2).

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Edema is symmetrical and bilateral, accompanied by ascites and elevated JVP indicating a central, systemic cause.

Liver Cirrhosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Ascites and hepatomegaly are accompanied by a loud P2, RV heave, and elevated JVP with v waves, specifically tying the hepatic congestion to right ventricular failure.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits cardinal signs of right ventricular failure (Cor Pulmonale) secondary to end-stage chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, manifesting as tender hepatomegaly, profound peripheral edema, right ventricular heave, and hypoxemia. The acute worsening of these congestive symptoms and resting hypoxemia represent a severe physiological decompensation. Urgent physical evaluation was required to initiate immediate diuresis and optimize oxygen therapy to decrease pulmonary vascular resistance, effectively preventing an inevitable emergency hospital admission due to respiratory or cardiac collapse.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered immediate dose of oral furosemide. Instructed strict compliance with continuous long-term oxygen therapy at 2L/min to combat hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction. Recommended urgent outpatient transthoracic echocardiogram.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instructed the patient to wear their prescribed nasal oxygen continuously for at least 15 hours a day, including overnight. By recognizing that oxygen directly reduces the strain on the right side of the heart, the patient connects compliance to a reduction in leg swelling. The reward is less abdominal bloating and improved leg comfort.
Home-Based Services: Arranged for community nursing review for edema management and medication compliance.
DME/Safety: Ensure home oxygen tubing is securely routed to prevent falls.
Follow-up: General Practice review in 48 hours for clinical reassessment and to review basic metabolic panel (electrolytes/renal function).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient develops worsening confusion, extreme drowsiness, increasing breathlessness despite oxygen, or an inability to speak in sentences, an ambulance must be called immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis relies on the physical signs of right heart strain; structural confirmation requires echocardiography.
Clinical Course: Cor Pulmonale represents advanced lung disease. Management focuses on treating the underlying lung hypoxia and carefully managing fluid overload.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Use your oxygen exactly as prescribed. Keep your legs elevated above your hips when resting. Seek emergency care if you become increasingly drowsy or breathless.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your lung disease has lowered your oxygen levels, causing the blood vessels in your lungs to tighten. This makes the right side of your heart work too hard, leading to a fluid backup in your liver and legs. Oxygen is the main treatment to relax those vessels.
Actionable Steps: Keep your oxygen on. Take the new fluid tablet in the morning. Elevate your legs when sitting.

